{
  "id": "praeteritum-plusquamperfekt",
  "name": "Narrative tenses: past continuous and past perfect",
  "level": "B1",
  "subtitle": "A story needs three tenses. Use the past simple for the main events: I opened the door. Use the past continuous for the background or an action in progress: It was raining. Use the past perfect for what happened earlier: Someone had broken the window.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "past simple",
    "past continuous",
    "past perfect"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "past perfect · had + past participle (earlier)",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "past simple · main events",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "past continuous · was / were + -ing (background)"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "When I <span class=\"vp-verb\">got</span> home, my brother <span class=\"vp-prep\">had left</span> and it was raining.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Past simple</strong> tells the <strong>main events</strong> of a story, one after the other: <em>I opened the door. I saw a cat. It ran away.</em>",
        "tag": "Main events"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>was / were + verb-ing</strong> describes the <strong>background</strong> or an action in progress: <em>It was raining. The children were playing.</em> Negative: <em>wasn't / weren't</em>. Question: <em>Was she sleeping?</em>",
        "tag": "Background: past continuous"
      },
      {
        "html": "A <strong>long action</strong> (past continuous) is interrupted by a <strong>short event</strong> (past simple): <em>I was cooking when the phone rang.</em> or <em>While I was cooking, the phone rang.</em>",
        "tag": "Interrupted action"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>had + past participle</strong> shows what happened <strong>earlier</strong> than another past event: <em>When I arrived, the film had started.</em> It is the same for every person: <em>I had, she had, they had</em> (short form: <em>I'd, she'd</em>). Negative: <em>hadn't</em>. Question: <em>Had you …?</em>",
        "tag": "Earlier event: past perfect"
      },
      {
        "html": "Compare the order: <em>When I got home, my brother <strong>left</strong>.</em> (he left after I came) — <em>When I got home, my brother <strong>had left</strong>.</em> (he was already gone).",
        "tag": "Order of events"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I was watching TV when she was calling.</em> → ✓ <em>I was watching TV when she called.</em> (the short event is past simple) · ✗ <em>When I got to the station, the train left.</em> (you mean it was gone) → ✓ <em>the train had left.</em> · ✗ <em>I had seen him yesterday.</em> → ✓ <em>I saw him yesterday.</em> (the past perfect needs a second, later past event)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "The past perfect almost never stands alone. It needs a second past event to look back from. Typical signals are <em>when, after, before, by the time, already</em> and <em>just</em>: <em>By the time we arrived, they had already eaten.</em> If <em>after</em> or <em>before</em> make the order clear, the past simple is also fine: <em>After I ate, I left.</em> State verbs (<em>know, like, want</em>) are not used in the continuous. For the forms of the past simple, see <a href=\"/past-simple\">Past simple</a>; for the present perfect, see <a href=\"/present-perfect\">Present perfect vs past simple</a>; for <em>when</em> and <em>while</em> clauses, see <a href=\"/time-clauses\">Time clauses</a>.",
